==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|de|Dolmetsch||archaic or Austria}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|tolmetsche}} (13th c.), ultimately from {{der|de|trk-pro|*tilmač}} (compare {{cog|tr|dilmaç}}). Probably borrowed through a {{der|de|sla|-}} language; compare  {{cog|sla-pro|*tъlmačь||interpreter}}, whence {{cog|ru|sc=Cyrl|толма́ч}}, {{cog|pl|tłumacz}}. Alternatively through {{der|de|hu|tolmács}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈdɔlmɛt͡ʃər/|[ˈdɔl.mɛ.t͡ʃɐ]|[-ˌmɛ-]}}
* {{audio|de|De-Dolmetscher.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-Dolmetscher2.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|m|f=in}}

# [[interpreter]] {{gloss|one who translates in real time; male or of unspecified sex}}
# {{lb|de|archaic}} [[translator]] {{q|male or of unspecified gender}}
#: {{syn|de|Übersetzer}}

====Usage notes====
* While it remains common in general parlance to use {{m|de||Übersetzer}} as an umbrella term, it is now unusual to refer to a translator of books or other completed texts as a {{m|de||Dolmetscher}}.

